CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 3 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness --memory-leak-check --pointer-check --alloc-check --no-assertions
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
A `pthread_create` immediately followed by its `pthread_join` is rewritten into
a plain call to the thread body (`simplify_pthread_create_join` in
`goto_convert_functions.cpp`). That is sound for the schedule -- nothing can
interleave between the two -- but not when the body ends in `pthread_exit`.

`pthread_exit`'s model ends in `__CPROVER_assume(0)`, which means "this thread
stops here" only while the state holds more than one thread: `symex_assume_l2`
then adds it to that thread's own guard. Run as an ordinary call the state has
a single thread, the other branch is taken, the assumption goes into the
*global* formula, and "this thread stops" becomes "this execution is
infeasible".

What that costs is not a missed counterexample but a verification that never
happens. Everything after the collapsed call is dropped before it reaches the
equation, including the whole tail of `__CPROVER__start` -- and the
memory-leak assertion always sits there, after the threads, so it is the check
most exposed. Before the fix this program produced an equation with **zero**
ASSERT steps and was reported SUCCESSFUL; so was
`pthread-memsafety/list1.i`, which has exactly this create/join/create/join
shape and is expected to fail. Stock cbmc 5.95.1 answers both correctly.

Both threads here end in `pthread_exit` so both are collapse candidates, and
the program is wrong twice over: `writer` makes the assertion false, and
`leaker` loses its allocation. Either one alone would catch a regression; the
test keeps both because they fail through different checks -- an assertion and
the end-of-program leak assertion -- and the bug hid both.

The fix refuses the rewrite when the thread body can reach `pthread_exit`,
directly or through a callee. A thread that ends with `return` is still
collapsed, which is the case the optimisation was written for.
